          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for treating an acute migraine, and when is prevention considered?",
          "answer": "Guidance on migraine treatment comes from one national headache society summary. The retrieved text covers acute treatment and when prevention starts. It does not rate how strong the evidence is for each option, and the summary reports no disagreement between authorities.\n\n**Acute treatment** (National headache society guidance)\n- Treat early, while pain is still mild. The guidance says response falls once an attack is well established.\n- Simple painkillers and anti-inflammatory drugs are the options for mild to moderate attacks.\n- Triptans are the options for moderate to severe attacks, or when simple painkillers have not worked.\n- Gepants and ditans are options where triptans don't work, are not tolerated, or are unsafe because of heart or blood-vessel disease. The guidance names this heart and blood-vessel limit as the main restriction on triptan use.\n- Anti-nausea medicines can be added where nausea makes swallowing tablets unreliable. Other routes are considered where vomiting or slow stomach emptying is a problem.\n- Guidance suggests judging a treatment only after two or three separate attacks, because attacks vary a lot.\n\nThe guidance groups these options by evidence strength rather than ranking them for any one person. The retrieved text does not say how strong that evidence is for each class, so I can't tell you that here.\n\n**When prevention is considered** (same document)\n- Prevention is offered when attacks are frequent or disabling enough that acute treatment alone is not enough.\n- The guidance gives a threshold of four or more migraine days a month with some disability. Fewer attacks can qualify if they are severely disabling, if acute treatment doesn't work or is not safe, or if the person prefers prevention.\n- The aim is fewer and milder attacks, not no attacks. A 50 percent cut in migraine days is the usual definition of a good response.\n\n**Limits of this summary**\n- The retrieved text does not cover the medication-overuse limits named in the document title, so I can't describe them.\n- No other authority was retrieved to compare against, so I can't report any disagreement on migraine.\n- Choosing and dosing a treatment is a conversation for a clinician who knows your history. Bring the guidance above, along with a record of how often attacks happen and how much they disrupt your day.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
